The current fix works for integer capacities, but the attached contract says only `capacity >= 0`. For fractional capacities, `q.length >= capacity` can still admit an item that exceeds capacity.

Boundary traces:
- `solve(0, [["push", 1], ["pop"]])`: push is rejected, queue stays empty; pop returns `null`. Actual and required: `[false, null]`.
- `solve(1, [["push", 1], ["push", 2], ["pop"], ["pop"]])`: first push fills the queue; second is rejected without changing it; pops return `1`, then `null`. Actual and required: `[true, false, 1, null]`.
- `solve(0.5, [["push", 1]])`: currently `0 >= 0.5` is false, so it admits the item and reaches length 1. Actual: `[true]`; required: `[false]`. The corrected check rejects it.
- `solve(2, [])`: no operations, so actual and required output are `[]`.

Complete replacement for `src/core.mjs`:

```js
export function solve(capacity, operations) {
  const q = [];
  const out = [];

  for (const [op, v] of operations) {
    if (op === "push") {
      if (q.length + 1 > capacity) {
        out.push(false);
      } else {
        q.push(v);
        out.push(true);
      }
    } else {
      out.push(q.length ? q.shift() : null);
    }
  }

  return out;
}
```

The restored invariant is `0 <= q.length <= capacity`: admission checks the resulting length, rejection leaves the queue unchanged, and popping preserves FIFO order. No dependencies, API changes, or test edits are needed; the existing `npm test` command remains unchanged.

